WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:06.540
In this next section, I want to do a quick overview of the ICD-FX camera and frustrum.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.660
Now, we set this up already in our end display configuration, but we need to talk a little

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.140
bit about the ICD-FX camera and frustrum if you're not familiar with these terms.

4
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.379
So understanding the way the inner frustrum and outer frustrum work on your end display

5
00:00:20.379 --> 00:00:23.719
configuration is really going to be important.

6
00:00:23.719 --> 00:00:26.899
The inner frustrum is the region of your LED wall that is actually going to show

7
00:00:26.899 --> 00:00:30.280
up on screen and in camera.

8
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.280
That's what your camera sees.

9
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.960
It's the actual field of view, the focal length, the zoom of your real camera.

10
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.439
This is enabled by creating your ICD-FX camera, as we did before, and connecting it to the

11
00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:43.539
live-length tracked camera, just as we did.

12
00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:46.380
Now the outer frustrum is everything else.

13
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:51.420
It's used for reflections, dynamic lighting, and it remains static while your camera moves

14
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.579
around.

15
00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:58.579
Now the engine does support multiple frustrums, but to set up your frustrum, I just want

16
00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:04.480
to review the steps to create your ICD-FX camera and create your tracked object.

17
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.680
So we're going to start out in the engine here inside our end display configuration.

18
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.319
So first thing I'm going to do is I'm going to edit my NDC asset, and I'm going

19
00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:17.519
to take a look here and point out that I've got my ICD-FX camera.

20
00:01:17.519 --> 00:01:20.579
Now I want to make sure that this matches, so I'm just going to go ahead and change

21
00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:21.640
the name here.

22
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.980
I'm going to use ICD-FX as the naming on this, and I'm going to change this to

23
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.819
match the camera we're actually using.

24
00:01:27.859 --> 00:01:33.540
So on stage, this is my Blackmagic ZEISS camera.

25
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.579
This way, I know what I'm actually connecting to.

26
00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:39.579
I'm going to go ahead and hit compile and save this.

27
00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:43.299
Now if I select this, you can see I've got to send a camera actor that's in here,

28
00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:47.319
and I've got several options that are available to me once that's ready.

29
00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:49.760
Now I'm going to hide this and come back to that, because they're going to see

30
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.920
some changes here.

31
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.159
Now this is my ICD-FX camera.

32
00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:59.200
With my end display configuration selected, if I select my ICD-FX camera underneath

33
00:01:59.239 --> 00:02:04.010
my NDC asset, you can see that this is the selected camera.

34
00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:09.330
Now I need something here to add to it, so I'm going to create some objects here.

35
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.409
I'm going to move up to my Place Actors panel, and I'm going to move into my

36
00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:15.969
basic section, and I'm going to add in an empty actor.

37
00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:20.729
Now this empty actor, I'm going to really quickly rename to Tracking Offset.

38
00:02:20.729 --> 00:02:25.370
Then I'm going to move this underneath my NDC asset.

39
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.330
Now if yours doesn't connect, it's because you're not actually in your end display

40
00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.930
config level.

41
00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.969
Remember, we are in the NDC level, not the persistent level, in order to add in our

42
00:02:34.969 --> 00:02:39.449
tracked offset in order to parent that underneath our NDC asset.

43
00:02:39.449 --> 00:02:44.009
Next, let's just zero out our location and our rotation information.

44
00:02:44.009 --> 00:02:51.259
This way, we're at our origin position that matches our offset inside motive.

45
00:02:51.259 --> 00:02:53.099
Now, I do need a camera here.

46
00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:56.259
That camera is going to take over for our NDC asset.

47
00:02:56.259 --> 00:03:00.219
If I zoom in really quickly, you can see this little pop here.

48
00:03:00.219 --> 00:03:05.110
This little pop-up where we've got some sky, that's our inner frustrum.

49
00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:10.110
If I select my NDC asset and I select my ICVFX camera that's listed in here,

50
00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.909
you can see I've got an Enable Inner Frostrum option checked on.

51
00:03:13.909 --> 00:03:18.750
If I turn that off, turn it back on, this section pops in and out.

52
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.710
Now I'm going to zoom back out a little bit, switch over in my Place Actors

53
00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.770
to the Cinematic tab.

54
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.569
I'm going to grab my Cine Camera Actor and just drag this into place,

55
00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:29.849
so it's in the level.

56
00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:41.050
Now, I'm going to rename this one to my NDC Blackmagic Zeiss Camera.

57
00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.169
And the reason I'm going to call it that is that this is the NDC's

58
00:03:44.169 --> 00:03:46.770
version of that camera.

59
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:51.689
Now, I'm going to go ahead and parent this underneath my tracking offset so

60
00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:56.830
that this takes all that information for calibration later.

61
00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.210
Now, I'm going to go ahead and zero this out.

62
00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:02.930
And then with my tracked object in here, I need to add a component

63
00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.650
to this camera.

64
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:08.449
That camera component that I'm going to add is with my Zeiss camera selected,

65
00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:13.680
hit Add Component, and add another Live Link Component Controller.

66
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.160
I'm going to change the name of this one to Live Link CC, so LLCC,

67
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.920
Live Link Component Controller.

68
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:26.379
I'm going to call this one Blackmagic Zeiss Track.

69
00:04:26.379 --> 00:04:27.860
It's for tracking the camera.

70
00:04:27.899 --> 00:04:29.779
That's what we're going to use this for.

71
00:04:29.779 --> 00:04:31.500
It's going to use my scene component.

72
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:36.139
I'm going to use my subject representation and choose my Blackmagic Active

73
00:04:36.139 --> 00:04:38.939
because I'm using my active tracking object.

74
00:04:38.939 --> 00:04:40.939
That's going to pop this into place.

75
00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:45.100
And once this is ready to go, I can move back up to my NDC asset

76
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.259
in my Outliner, choose my ICVFX Camera, and right under here where I've got

77
00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:55.779
my Cine Camera Actor, I can select my Blackmagic Zeiss Camera so that my

78
00:04:55.819 --> 00:05:00.939
inner frustum is now being run through this camera and not the ICVFX Camera,

79
00:05:00.939 --> 00:05:03.370
which you'll note disappears.

80
00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:09.350
Now, I can also enable an inner frustum border, which if I change to 0.1,

81
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.110
you can start to see show up.

82
00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:15.029
0.5 makes it thick enough so that it actually shows up on screen.

83
00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:17.470
But I'm going to settle it at 0.25.

84
00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.819
It's just enough to see but not enough to be in the way.

85
00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:23.300
Now, I can also change my border color.

86
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.379
So, as I know, Sean is a little partial to green.

87
00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:29.279
Let's go ahead and make a nice little green there.

88
00:05:29.279 --> 00:05:31.399
And that's our outline.

89
00:05:31.399 --> 00:05:34.720
Now, there is also some overscan options.

90
00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.839
If I enable overscan for my frustum, I can also make sure that this

91
00:05:38.839 --> 00:05:40.240
shows up on screen.

92
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.720
And if I change that to 1.2, my frustum gets bigger.

93
00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.319
This way, if my subject is in front of my camera, I can make sure that my

94
00:05:47.319 --> 00:05:52.870
frustum aligns to outside of my camera's view and there is no tearing.

95
00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.430
That way, if I have any kind of move or anything like that,

96
00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:59.189
this will actually give me a bit of a buffer so that as my camera moves,

97
00:05:59.189 --> 00:06:02.470
it kind of goes through and follows along.

98
00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:07.949
Now, let's jump back to our slides and let's talk about your ICD-FX camera

99
00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:10.100
and inner frustums.

100
00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:14.220
Now, the ICD-FX camera does support multiple frustums.

101
00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.779
You just add in, the same as we did before, another camera,

102
00:06:17.779 --> 00:06:21.660
you iterate that or give it the same name of a new camera that you're using,

103
00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.860
and then track that camera in there as well.

104
00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:30.779
You can also use the Live Link V-Cam with a Pixel streaming server to

105
00:06:30.779 --> 00:06:35.980
generate a second camera and use that as well for more virtual camera work.

106
00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:40.860
Note, as you move through your NDC camera with the two inner frustums,

107
00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.779
it is recommended that you limit yourself to just two frustums.

108
00:06:45.779 --> 00:06:49.899
You can also set the priority of your frustum so that the main frustum for

109
00:06:49.939 --> 00:06:52.779
your camera is the one that overlaps.

110
00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:57.949
That way, your secondary camera doesn't necessarily override that as well.

111
00:06:57.949 --> 00:07:01.149
So that finishes up our discussion on cameras,

112
00:07:01.149 --> 00:07:03.470
and we are ready to move on to our next section.
